.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIlibmpq\fP reads and creates MPQ v1/v2/v3/v4 archives, including embedded
MPQ containers. V3 creation emits both HET/BET and classic tables.
V3/v4 transactions can retain unrelated unnamed members with sufficient
stored identities; see libmpq__update_begin(3) for restrictions.
.SS "Archive Opening and Custom I/O"
Open filesystem archives with \fBlibmpq__archive_open\fP(3) or provide a
borrowed random-access callback through \fBlibmpq__archive_open_io\fP(3).
A negative archive offset enables embedded-header scanning. Clones own
independent read state; close each archive handle when finished.
.SS "Logical Member Streams"
Use \fBlibmpq__stream_open\fP(3) or
\fBlibmpq__stream_open_name\fP(3) for incremental decoded reads and
seeking. Streams own a private archive clone and are consumed by
\fBlibmpq__stream_close\fP(3). Complete reads use
\fBlibmpq__file_read\fP(3).
Patch-file members instead expose their decoded, unapplied PTCH bodies through
\fBlibmpq__patch_payload_size\fP(3) and
\fBlibmpq__patch_payload_read\fP(3).
.SS "Archive Creation and Writers"
Create MPQ v1/v2/v3/v4 archives with \fBlibmpq__archive_create\fP(3).
Use \fBlibmpq__archive_create_io\fP(3) for a fresh caller-owned sink.
Use \fBlibmpq__archive_create_mpqe_io\fP(3) to send MPQE transport to an
initially empty caller-owned sink at archive close. A private plaintext MPQ
stage handles all read-back for integrity and signing.
Add complete buffers or paths with the archive-add calls, or use the
writer begin/write/finish lifecycle for streamed members. Writer finish
consumes the member writer; archive close finalizes and consumes the
archive handle. V4 writers default to raw chunks of 0x4000 bytes; the archive
and patch raw-size setters configure the policy before writing or signing.
Their getters report configured or loaded values, with zero for older formats.
Encrypted random-access writing is not supported.
.SS "Transactional Updates"
\fBlibmpq__update_begin\fP(3) stages modifications to ordinary or
embedded MPQs in a distinct handle. Authenticated MPQE updates use
\fBlibmpq__update_begin_mpqe\fP(3). Add, replace, remove, and rename affect
only the working copy until commit atomically publishes it; abort
discards it. Both commit and abort consume the update handle.
Addition requires an absent staged filename; its file options define the
created identity. Ordinary replacement preserves its existing lookup and mutation behavior.
The explicit identity operations select a locale/platform tuple without
fallback, including (0,0), and preserve unrelated aliases. Replacement
retains the selected tuple; explicit storage options must match it. libmpq__update_change_identity(3) selects the old
locale/platform tuple explicitly and changes it to the new tuple without
changing the filename or payload.
V3/v4 rebuilds require sufficient lookup identities and supported metadata;
unsupported inputs fail rather than silently omit files. Modified signatures
are removed.
.SS "Patch Archives"
The patch creation API stages ordinary additions, replacements and deletion
entries without changing the base archive. Finish publishes a new patch
artifact; abort discards it, and both consume the patch handle. Ordinary and
MPQE-wrapped patch artifacts have separate begin calls.
.PP
libmpq__archive_open_patch(3) synchronously composes already-open base and
ordered patch archives into an independently owned read-only archive. Zero
layers produce an independent snapshot; explicit addition-only overlays are
supported. Inputs are borrowed, and the result has the usual archive, clone
and stream lifetimes. Physical input signatures and raw checksums are not
inherited as trust in the logical result.
.SS Signatures
\fBlibmpq__archive_signatures\fP(3) detects stored signatures;
\fBlibmpq__archive_verify\fP(3) verifies one type using a caller-supplied
public key. Writer signing is configured with
\fBlibmpq__archive_sign\fP(3) or \fBlibmpq__patch_sign\fP(3) and
performed at finalization. No default or product-specific keys are
built in.
.SS MPQE
MPQE uses the supplied code for transport decoding and encryption. It has
no independent authentication tag. Successful decoding and MPQ structural
checks do not guarantee that a wrong code was detected. Member checksums
and archive signatures are separate checks.
Internal weak signatures work in MPQE-wrapped MPQs, subject to the existing
rejection for v4 output with nonzero raw-chunk size. No external strong-signature
transport is defined. Creation and transactional updates use private plaintext
staging files; cleanup after abnormal termination is best effort.
.SS "Metadata and Integrity"
\fBlibmpq__archive_verify_ex\fP(3) freshly compares stored header/table MD5
references and HET/BET trailing RAW arrays against opened descriptors, using
LIBMPQ_VERIFY_METADATA_* flags. It reports checked, unavailable and mismatch
masks. This differs from signature-only archive_verify() and member-level
file_verify_ex(); none alone guarantees whole-archive integrity or trust.
Archive, file, and block queries expose sizes, flags, and version-100
\fB(attributes)\fP rows. Complete lossless file reads automatically
check available CRC32/MD5 and usable sector Adler-32 data.
\fBlibmpq__file_verify_ex\fP(3) reports checked, unavailable and mismatch
member checks; the optional name is key context, not selection.
\fBlibmpq__file_verify\fP(3) performs selected explicit comparisons;
lossy ADPCM may legitimately differ from source-byte checksums.
libmpq__file_identity(3) enumerates filename identities and their CLASSIC/BET
provenance without merging locale/platform aliases. Explicit identity
file-number lookup and named streams select exactly, including neutral (0,0).
Ordinary operations retain HET-first/classic first-hit resolution.
.SH THREAD SAFETY AND OWNERSHIP
Serialize operations on each handle. Clones and streams have their own
handles but may share non-atomic references to temporary sources and
verification state. Externally serialize operations on related handles that
acquire or release shared references, including cloning, lazy verification
and closing. Keep borrowed custom I/O contexts alive until the last
dependent handle closes and synchronize shared callback access.
Library-owned temporary sources can remain live until the last owner closes.
A consumed close, commit or abort handle cannot be reused, including when
the consuming operation returns an error.
.PP
Canonical file numbers belong to the opened representation. Resolve names
or identities again after reopening an archive rebuilt by a transaction.
.PP
\fBLIBMPQ_VERIFY_ALL\fP expands to 0xF in the current header. Applications
built with v0.8.1 retain their compiled-in 0x7 mask. Recompiling adds
\fBLIBMPQ_VERIFY_RAW_MD5\fP, which can add work, be unavailable or return
an error. This changes recompiled caller behavior without changing an ELF
symbol or function signature.
